(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-physiology-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form A","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which hypothalamic-hypophysial regulatory hormone stimulates TSH secretion by thyrotropes?","choices":["Corticotropin-releasing hormone","Thyrotropin-releasing hormone","Growth hormone-releasing hormone","Gonadotropin-releasing hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), a peptide, stimulates TSH secretion by thyrotropes. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Epinephrine, a hormone of the adrenal medulla, acts on which tissues per this lecture?","choices":["The thyroid gland exclusively","Skin only","Muscle, liver, and fat","Bone and cartilage only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Epinephrine acts on muscle, liver, and fat. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Luteinizing hormone (LH), from the anterior pituitary, regulates spermatogenesis by stimulating which cells to secrete testosterone?","choices":["Epididymal cells","Sertoli cells","Spermatogonia directly","Leydig cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"LH stimulates Leydig cells to secrete testosterone. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Mesangial cells are best described as:","choices":["Specialized cells found exclusively within the epithelial walls of the renal collecting duct system","Cells that are NOT part of the filtration barrier, but contain contractile actin/myosin microfilaments that may alter capillary blood flow","A required fourth structural layer that completes and reinforces the glomerular filtration barrier itself","The primary anatomical site where erythropoietin hormone is synthesized and released into the blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Mesangial cells are not part of the glomerular filtration barrier itself; they produce extracellular matrix for structural support and contain actin/myosin-based microfilaments that contact the basement membrane and may alter blood flow through the capillaries. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which of the following increases aldosterone secretion?","choices":["A decrease in plasma K+","Angiotensin II","Atrial natriuretic factor (ANF)","Increased Na+ concentration (osmolality)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Angiotensin II, along with increased K+ levels and ACTH, increases aldosterone secretion. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which structure is described as the smallest contractile unit of a skeletal muscle fiber?","choices":["The myofibril","The sarcolemma","The sarcomere","The muscle fiber"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the slides state the sarcomere is the smallest contractile unit of a muscle fiber. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 5"},{"q":"In the lecture's example of negative feedback, angiotensin II inhibits which upstream component of its own cascade?","choices":["Renin","Aldosterone secretion","ACE","Angiotensinogen production by the liver directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"In the negative-feedback example given, angiotensin II feeds back to inhibit renin. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which GI hormone, alongside secretin, is listed as affecting insulin secretion?","choices":["Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)","Cholecystokinin (CCK)","Motilin, a GI motility hormone","Gastrin, a stomach-derived hormone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1), alongside secretin, is listed among the GI hormones affecting insulin secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Progesterone's effect on the fallopian tubes is to promote secretion by the mucosal lining, which serves what purpose?","choices":["Has no functional purpose","Provides a nutrient source for the ovum","Dissolves the ovum before fertilization","Prevents sperm from entering the fallopian tube"],"answer":1,"exp":"Progesterone-driven mucosal secretion in the fallopian tubes provides a nutrient source for the ovum. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"The perforations in the glomerular capillary endothelium are called:","choices":["Filtration slits","Slit diaphragms","Fenestrae","Pedicels"],"answer":2,"exp":"The glomerular capillary endothelium has thousands of perforations called fenestrae. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Secretion, as one of the basic mechanisms of urine formation, is defined as:","choices":["Useful materials being reabsorbed into the blood from the tubules","Plasma being filtered to remove water and small solutes","The remainder being excreted as urine","Substances being moved from the blood into the renal tubules"],"answer":3,"exp":"Secretion is the movement of substances from the blood into the renal tubules. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which of the following is the correct definition of a motor unit?","choices":["The specialized synapse between a motoneuron and a muscle fiber","A bundle of myofibrils contained within one muscle fiber","A group of motor neurons innervating one muscle fiber","A collection of muscle fibers innervated by a single motor neuron"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a motor unit is a collection of muscle fibers innervated by one motor neuron. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 34"},{"q":"The decrease in GH-driven protein catabolism is suggested to be related to which other GH metabolic action?","choices":["Decreased amino acid transport","Decreased RNA transcription","Increased fat utilization","Increased carbohydrate utilization"],"answer":2,"exp":"The decrease in protein catabolism under GH is suggested to possibly be related to GH's increased fat utilization (sparing protein by favoring fat as an energy source). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which set of findings is associated with HIGH aldosterone levels?","choices":["Hypokalemia, muscle weakness, slow heart rate/arrhythmias, cell alkalosis","No change in potassium, heart rate, or acid-base status","Hypercalcemia, diffuse bone pain, and recurrent pathologic fractures","Hyperkalemia, increased muscle excitability, cell acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"High aldosterone is associated with hypokalemia, muscle weakness, slow heart rate/arrhythmias, and cell alkalosis. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"About how long does it take for the fertilized ovum to travel and enter the uterus as a blastocyst?","choices":["A few hours","3–5 days","1–2 months","2–3 weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"It takes a total of about 3–5 days for the fertilized ovum to enter the uterus as a blastocyst (about 100 cells). — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Why is the micturition reflex described as 'self-regenerative'?","choices":["Because once the first contraction is triggered, the entire cycle continues entirely on its own without requiring any further neural input whatsoever.","Because each new contraction cycle is carried out through an entirely different, previously unused nerve pathway to the bladder wall each time.","Because an initial contraction further stimulates stretch receptors, increasing sensory impulses that increase reflex contractions, repeating until the bladder achieves a strong contraction","Because the bladder wall's detrusor smooth muscle tissue is physically regenerated and structurally repaired after each complete voiding event."],"answer":2,"exp":"The reflex is self-regenerative because an initial contraction activates stretch receptors, further increasing sensory impulses from the bladder/posterior urethra, which further increases reflex contractions — a cycle that repeats until the bladder reaches a strong degree of contraction. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which nephron segment is described as functionally similar to the thick ascending limb, impermeable to water, and the 'diluting' segment?","choices":["Collecting duct","Proximal straight tubule","Early distal tubule","Late distal tubule"],"answer":2,"exp":"The early distal tubule functions like the thick ascending limb in some ways, is not permeable to water (making it the 'diluting' segment), and actively reabsorbs Na+, Cl-, K+, and Mg++. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Acetylcholine (ACh) binds nicotinic receptor channels to produce an end plate potential. What normally terminates this signaling process at the motor end plate?","choices":["Reuptake of acetylcholine into the nerve terminal","Closure of the voltage-gated sodium channels","Pumping of calcium back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Breakdown of acetylcholine by acetylcholinesterase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: acetylcholinesterase degrades acetylcholine at the motor end plate, terminating the process. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 12"},{"q":"Per the lecture's histologic staining classification, which anterior pituitary cell types are basophilic?","choices":["Mammotropes and gonadotropes only","Thyrotropes, gonadotropes, and corticotropes","Somatotropes, mammotropes, and thyrotropes","Somatotropes and corticotropes only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thyrotropes, gonadotropes, and corticotropes are classified as basophilic cell types. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Besides epinephrine, hypoglycemia also increases which two other hormones that promote fat utilization and decrease glucose utilization?","choices":["Insulin and glucagon","Aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone","TSH, ACTH, and prolactin","Growth hormone and cortisol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hypoglycemia increases growth hormone and cortisol, which promote fat utilization and decrease glucose utilization, complementing epinephrine's response. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"During pregnancy, aldosterone approximately doubles, contributing to:","choices":["Sodium and water retention","No change in fluid balance","Sodium and water loss","Only potassium retention, with no sodium effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"A twofold increase in aldosterone during pregnancy contributes to sodium and water retention. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Compared to typical capillary membranes elsewhere in the body, the glomerular capillary membrane's Kf is approximately:","choices":["About 400 times greater","About the same as typical capillaries","About 4 times greater","About 40 times greater"],"answer":0,"exp":"The glomerular capillary membrane's Kf is roughly 400 times greater than in many other tissues, letting it filter several hundred times more water and solutes than a typical capillary membrane. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which transporters are found in principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct?","choices":["Carbonic anhydrase and NH3 transporter only","Aquaporin-1 and Na+/glucose cotransporter only","H+ ATPase, H+/K+ ATPase, Cl-/HCO3- exchanger","ENaC, ROMK, Na+/K+ ATPase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Principal cells contain ENaC, ROMK, and Na+/K+ ATPase, supporting their function of Na+ reabsorption, ADH-dependent H2O reabsorption, and K+ secretion. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"After skeletal muscle contraction, calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, where it binds which protein to facilitate storage?","choices":["Nebulin","Troponin C","Calmodulin","Calsequestrin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: calcium binds calsequestrin inside the reticulum to facilitate its storage. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which anterior pituitary cell type produces both follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone?","choices":["Thyrotropes","Somatotropes","Gonadotropes","Corticotropes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gonadotropes produce both FSH and LH. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Besides direct glucose levels, which factor listed also stimulates insulin release via adrenergic mechanisms?","choices":["Cold environmental exposure exclusively, with no other trigger","Only somatostatin, acting alone as an inhibitor","Only glucose levels, with no other described stimulus","Adrenergic stimulation (norepinephrine, epinephrine)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Adrenergic stimulation (via norepinephrine and epinephrine) is listed among the factors affecting insulin secretion, alongside glucose, somatostatin, GI hormones (secretin, GLP-1), free fatty acids, and ketoacids. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Estrogen's electrolyte effect is a slight increase in retention of which substances?","choices":["Chloride only, with no effect on sodium","Phosphate and magnesium","Sodium and water","Potassium and calcium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Estrogen has a slight effect to increase sodium and water retention. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"According to the lecture's innervation summary table, which nerve originates from S2–S4 and drives detrusor contraction?","choices":["Pudendal nerve","Pelvic nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Genitofemoral nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pelvic nerve, originating from S2–S4, drives detrusor contraction. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"In the RAAS sequence, what converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II?","choices":["Renin (proteolytic enzyme)","Aldosterone (adrenal hormone)","ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme)","ADH (antidiuretic hormone)"],"answer":2,"exp":"ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) converts angiotensin I into angiotensin II. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Chondrocytes in the epiphyseal growth plates are primarily regulated by:","choices":["Parathyroid hormone from the parathyroid glands","Insulin-like growth factor I (IGF-I) produced by the liver","Calcitonin secreted by the parafollicular C-cells of the thyroid","Cathepsin K released by activated osteoclasts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: growth-plate chondrocytes are regulated by IGF-I, made by the liver in response to growth hormone. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 59"},{"q":"Growth hormone's three broad metabolic actions include increased protein synthesis, increased fat utilization, and:","choices":["Decreased carbohydrate utilization","Decreased protein synthesis","Increased carbohydrate utilization","No effect on carbohydrate metabolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Growth hormone's three broad metabolic actions are increased protein synthesis, increased fat utilization, and DEcreased carbohydrate utilization. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What percentage of circulating cortisol is bound to corticosteroid binding globulin (CBG)?","choices":["About 25%","About 10%","About 90%","About 60%"],"answer":2,"exp":"About 90% of circulating cortisol is bound to corticosteroid binding globulin. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Braxton-Hicks contractions are best described as:","choices":["Contractions that only occur during the second stage of labor","Weak, slow, irritable uterine muscle contractions beginning about 1 month before labor","Strong, rhythmic uterine contractions that are entirely identical to true active labor","A reliable sign indicating that delivery will occur within the hour"],"answer":1,"exp":"Braxton-Hicks contractions are weak, slow contractions reflecting uterine muscle irritability, beginning about 1 month before true labor. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"The glomerular capillary filtration coefficient (Kf) is the product of:","choices":["Renal blood flow rate and afferent arteriole vessel diameter","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure and plasma osmotic pressure","Hydraulic conductivity (permeability) and filtering surface area","Plasma protein concentration and molecular size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Kf is the product of the permeability (hydraulic conductivity) and the filtering surface area of the glomerular capillaries. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"ANP dilates the afferent arteriole and constricts the efferent arteriole. What is the net effect on GFR?","choices":["GFR increases","GFR decreases","GFR becomes unmeasurable","GFR is unaffected"],"answer":0,"exp":"By dilating the afferent arteriole and constricting the efferent arteriole, ANP increases glomerular hydrostatic pressure and increases GFR. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"As defined in the lecture, a syncytium is:","choices":["A bundle of discrete cells each with its own separate innervation","A network of cells joined only by mechanical desmosome junctions","A single cell containing one centrally located nucleus","A multinucleated mass of protoplasm produced by the merging of cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: this is the lecture's exact definition of a syncytium. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 40"},{"q":"What is thyroid hormone's effect on serum cholesterol at high (versus low) T3/T4 levels?","choices":["High T3/T4 decreases serum cholesterol; low T3/T4 increases it","High T3/T4 increases serum cholesterol; low T3/T4 decreases it","Both high and low T3/T4 increase serum cholesterol equally","Thyroid hormone has no effect on serum cholesterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"High T3/T4 is associated with increased lipolysis and DEcreased serum cholesterol, while low T3/T4 is associated with decreased lipolysis and increased serum cholesterol. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which cell type is specifically referenced in the lecture's figure illustrating aldosterone's physiological actions on potassium/acid-base balance?","choices":["Intercalated cell","Podocyte foot process","Mesangial cell","Principal cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"The intercalated cell is specifically referenced in the figure illustrating aldosterone's physiological actions (relevant to the acid-base consequences of high/low aldosterone). — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Compared to testosterone, estrogen's effect on protein deposition is:","choices":["A much stronger increase than testosterone's protein effect","Exactly identical in magnitude to testosterone's protein effect","A net decrease in overall protein deposition throughout the body","A slight increase, not as strong as testosterone's"],"answer":3,"exp":"Estrogen causes a slight increase in protein deposition, though not as strong an effect as testosterone's. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which proteins found in the podocyte filtration slit diaphragm help restrict the passage of plasma proteins?","choices":["Actin and myosin","Collagen and elastin","Aquaporin and uromodulin","Nephrin and podocin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nephrin and podocin are proteins in the slit diaphragm that help restrict the flow of plasma proteins across the filtration barrier. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Where is aldosterone released from once angiotensin II binds its receptors in the RAAS sequence?","choices":["Thyroid gland","Adrenal medulla","Adrenal cortex","Posterior pituitary"],"answer":2,"exp":"Once angiotensin II binds its receptors, aldosterone is released from the adrenal cortex. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"In skeletal muscle, the ryanodine receptor functions as which of the following?","choices":["The calcium release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum","The voltage-sensing dihydropyridine receptor of the T-tubule membrane","The ATP-driven calcium pump of the reticulum","The calcium-binding storage protein of the reticulum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the ryanodine receptor is the sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium release channel. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 17"},{"q":"Besides growth hormone-releasing hormone, which other hypothalamic factor specifically regulates GH secretion?","choices":["Corticotropin-releasing hormone","Thyrotropin-releasing hormone","Growth hormone-inhibitory hormone","Gonadotropin-releasing hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Growth hormone-inhibitory hormone is the other hypothalamic peptide that specifically regulates GH secretion, inhibiting it (opposite to GHRH's stimulatory role). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"During hypoglycemia, which hormone increases via sympathetic nerve stimulation to raise glucose from the liver?","choices":["Aldosterone","Epinephrine","Insulin","Oxytocin"],"answer":1,"exp":"During hypoglycemia, sympathetic nerve stimulation increases epinephrine, which increases glucose release from the liver. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"During early puberty (about ages 9–12), what happens to LH and FSH levels?","choices":["They decrease steadily","They increase steadily","They spike once and then permanently shut off","They remain completely flat with no change"],"answer":1,"exp":"LH and FSH levels increase steadily starting around ages 9–12 as part of pubertal onset. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which three variables determine glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG), per this lecture?","choices":["Respiratory rate, tidal volume, and total alveolar minute ventilation","Cardiac output, hematocrit percentage, and core body temperature reading","Plasma glucose concentration, plasma sodium concentration, and plasma potassium concentration","Arterial pressure, afferent arteriolar resistance, and efferent arteriolar resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Arterial pressure, afferent arteriolar resistance, and efferent arteriolar resistance are the three variables that determine glomerular hydrostatic pressure. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"During low blood pressure or decreased renal perfusion, angiotensin II preferentially constricts which vessel to preserve GFR?","choices":["Efferent arteriole","Afferent arteriole","Renal vein","Vasa recta"],"answer":0,"exp":"Angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole, helping maintain glomerular hydrostatic pressure and preserve GFR during hypovolemia. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which statement about parathyroid hormone (PTH) and plasma calcium is correct?","choices":["PTH is released when calcium-sensing receptors detect low serum calcium","PTH is released whenever serum calcium rises above the normal reference range","Protein-bound calcium is the only biologically active form","Roughly 60% of plasma calcium is protein bound and biologically inactive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: calcium-sensing receptors in the parathyroid gland trigger PTH release when serum calcium falls. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which hormone, released by neurons under direct hypothalamic control (bypassing the anterior pituitary regulatory-hormone route), acts on uterine smooth muscle and mammary glands in females?","choices":["Corticotropin","Follitropin","Thyrotropin","Oxytocin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Oxytocin, released directly from the posterior pituitary under direct hypothalamic neural control, acts on uterine smooth muscle and mammary glands in females (and on ductus deferens/prostate smooth muscle in males). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which two GI hormones are named as stimulating glucagon secretion?","choices":["VIP and somatostatin","Motilin and gastrin-releasing peptide","Cholecystokinin (CCK) and gastrin","Secretin and GLP-1"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) and gastrin are named as GI hormones that stimulate glucagon secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"During pregnancy, thyroxine can increase by up to what percentage, driven partly by hCG and human chorionic thyrotropin?","choices":["50%","300%","150%","5%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Thyroxine can increase by up to 50% during pregnancy, caused by hCG and human chorionic thyrotropin. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"During voluntary urination, what happens to the external sphincter at the same time the abdominal muscles contract to raise bladder pressure?","choices":["It contracts further and more tightly to actively prevent leakage","It is simultaneously relaxed to allow urine to pass","It is denervated during voluntary micturition","It has no involvement in voluntary urination"],"answer":1,"exp":"While abdominal muscle contraction raises bladder pressure and excites the micturition reflex via stretch, the external sphincter is simultaneously relaxed so urine can pass. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"The macula densa's primary sensing function within the JGA is to detect:","choices":["NaCl delivery","Plasma osmolality directly","Blood glucose concentration","Arterial oxygen saturation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The macula densa senses NaCl delivery to the distal tubule, driving tubuloglomerular feedback and renin release. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which statement about osteocytes is correct?","choices":["They are large multinucleated cells that actively resorb and remove mineralized bone","They respond to mechanical (piezoelectric) stimuli and maintain the bone matrix","They are immature dividing cells found only within growth plates","They secrete parathyroid hormone into the blood in response to low serum calcium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: osteocytes sense mechanical/piezoelectric stimuli and are responsible for maintaining the matrix. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which nine peptide/protein hormones make up the 'KEY TO PITUITARY HORMONES' listed together in this lecture's overview figure?","choices":["Cortisol, aldosterone, and the six anterior hormones","ACTH, TSH, GH, PRL, FSH, LH, MSH, ADH, and Oxytocin","Only ACTH, TSH, and GH","Insulin, glucagon, and PTH plus the six anterior hormones"],"answer":1,"exp":"The overview figure's key lists ACTH, TSH, GH, PRL, FSH, LH, MSH (all anterior), plus ADH and oxytocin (posterior) as the full set of pituitary hormones. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which amino acids are specifically named as stimulating glucagon secretion?","choices":["Arginine and alanine","Glycine and proline","Methionine and cysteine","Tryptophan and lysine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Arginine and alanine are the amino acids specifically named as stimulating glucagon secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Placental progesterone is synthesized primarily from:","choices":["Fetal androgens","Placental estrogen","hCG, a glycoprotein hormone","Maternal cholesterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Placental progesterone is synthesized primarily from maternal cholesterol. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Why can a single action potential cause the entire detrusor muscle to contract as a unit?","choices":["It is composed of skeletal muscle fibers under direct, conscious voluntary neural control at all times","The detrusor completely lacks gap junctions, so contraction is instead purely hormonal","Low-resistance electrical pathways between fused smooth muscle cells let the action potential spread from cell to cell","Each individual detrusor cell has its own independent nerve fiber firing in synchrony"],"answer":2,"exp":"Detrusor smooth muscle fibers extend in all directions and fuse with one another, with low-resistance electrical pathways between cells, so an action potential spreads throughout the muscle and the whole bladder contracts at once. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Extraglomerular mesangial cells, as part of the JGA, primarily contribute to:","choices":["Direct secretion of ADH hormone","Communication between JGA structures","Direct secretion of ADH into the tubule","Direct reabsorption of filtered glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Extraglomerular mesangial cells are part of the JGA and are involved in communication between its structures (macula densa and granular cells). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which subunit of the troponin complex is the one that binds calcium?","choices":["Tropomyosin","Troponin T","Troponin I","Troponin C"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: troponin C is the calcium-binding subunit of the troponin complex. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 22"}]};
